preConditionHook:
// THE OPENING RANGE -- the corpus's session primitive. The highest high and
// lowest low of the session's first openingBars bars fix orHigh, orLow and
// orHeight; every other opening-range rule INLINES this same block rather
// than referencing the file, as the corpus does with named composites.
//
// SESSION MODEL, per the standing decision: a session starts when the
// wall-clock close date changes -- day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1)),
// the EasyLanguage twin's date > date[1]. EL_Date_Probe.txt measured Date as
// the bar's wall-clock close date on 169/169 bars and the same slicing serves the xxxD
// family, so there is no session-date reconstruction anywhere. barsIn is 1 on
// the session's FIRST bar, the convention OpeningRangeBreakout and
// FailedBreakoutOfPriorSessionExtreme already use, so the range is still
// growing while barsIn <= openingBars and is FIXED for the rest of the
// session from the bar where barsIn reaches openingBars.
//
// As a filter the rule says one thing: the opening range is SET. True from
// the openingBars'th bar of the session to the session's last bar, false
// before it. Direction-agnostic, so both sides are identical text.
//
// WARM-UP: barsIn, orHigh and orLow start at 0 exactly as the EasyLanguage
// variables do, so if the first calculated bar falls mid-session the values
// describe a partial session until the first date change. That is EL's
// behaviour, mirrored rather than corrected. Intraday bars only.
if (day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1))) {
    barsIn = 1;
    orHigh = high[0];
    orLow = low[0];
} else {
    barsIn = barsIn + 1;
    if (barsIn <= (int)openingBars) {
        if (el_gt(high[0], orHigh)) orHigh = high[0];
        if (el_lt(low[0], orLow)) orLow = low[0];
    }
}
orHeight = orHigh - orLow;
orSet = el_ge((double)barsIn, openingBars);
